WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:02.950
2019 was a great year for movies.

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:07.130
Whether you bought tickets to see a raunchy
tween-starring comedy or a nuanced familial

00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:09.520
dramedy, it felt like there was a movie for
everyone.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.240
Here are the very best films of 2019.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:14.800
Rocketman is not a traditional biopic.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:19.290
It instead aims to capture the life of the
legendary Elton John through a deft blending

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:20.580
of fact and fantasy.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:25.680
It's a bold gambit that somehow works swimmingly,
thanks in large part to a brilliant lead performance

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:26.700
from Taron Egerton.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:31.520
Chicago Reader's Leah Pickett opined that
the fantasy conceit is part of what made the

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:32.500
film work so well.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:33.000
She wrote:

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:37.480
"This fantastical take...is undoubtedly how
the unconventional artist at its center wanted

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:38.140
it to be.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:43.160
The story reshuffles reality, especially time
and facts, and the film is more enjoyable

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:43.760
for it."

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:48.300
Many critics were complimentary of Egerton's
performance, but Quad City Times' Linda Cook

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.940
summed it up best with a sweet line:

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:53.829
"I hope you don't mind that I put down in
words how wonderful life is when Elton John's

00:00:53.829 --> 00:00:55.540
music, and this movie, are in the world."

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:00.840
"Never told you how much I need you."

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:02.520
"No, no, I love you mate."

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:06.380
Fans waited four long years for the ninth
film from Quentin Tarantino, and as usual,

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:10.240
the master delivered a singular and amazing
piece of cinema.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:15.020
Once Upon a Time in Hollywood is Tarantino's
portrait of the swinging '60s in Los Angeles,

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:19.210
and how they came to an abrupt end the night
of August 8, 1969.

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:23.370
The night that followers of the infamous Charles
Manson visited the house of pregnant actress

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:26.580
Sharon Tate, leaving the town changed forever
in their bloody wake.

00:01:26.689 --> 00:01:31.119
Tarantino's story is told through the lens
of fictional washed-up actor Rick Dalton and

00:01:31.119 --> 00:01:35.390
his best buddy and stunt double Cliff Booth,
as they find themselves in the middle of the

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:36.800
seismic events of that night.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:41.660
Praise was effusive for Brad Pitt and Leonardo
DiCaprio's "amazing" onscreen chemistry, and

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:45.789
while not every critic was convinced, Once
Upon a Time in Hollywood was generally found

00:01:45.789 --> 00:01:47.700
to stand with Tarantino's best work.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.620
As Lou Thomas of Den of Geek wrote:

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:54.120
"Tarantino has created a storming hangout
movie for the ages: changing times captured

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.880
remarkably by a film in love with cinema itself."

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:58.540
"I'm Rick Dalton's stunt double."

00:01:59.240 --> 00:01:59.820
"Stuntman."

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:01.380
"Yeah."

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.380
"You know, you're kind of pretty for a stuntman."

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:05.960
"That's what they tell me."

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:11.900
Writer/director Ari Aster made a huge noise
in 2018 with his terrifying debut Hereditary,

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.990
but in 2019, we discovered that he had just
been warming up.

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:20.300
Midsommar finds troubled couple Dani and Christian
going on a trek through the Swedish countryside

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.380
to a remote village.

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:25.680
The bizarre happenings there unfold not in
the dead of night, but in broad, sunny daylight,

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.160
one of many unusual aspects of the film which
critics singled out for praise.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:34.000
Midsommar's underlying themes also packed
a punch, as Dani's grief over a family tragedy

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:38.760
opens the door to a harrowing examination
of our ability to deal with internal struggles.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:40.240
Salon's Matthew Rosza wrote:

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:45.000
"Midsommar isn't just a great horror movie,
or proof that director Ari Aster is a budding

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.160
auteur who likely has a bright future ahead
of him.

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:50.880
It is also one of the best movies ever made
about living with mental illness."

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.440
From producer Guillermo del Toro and director
André Øvredal comes the big-screen adaptation

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:59.980
of the beloved childrens' book series Scary
Stories to Tell in the Dark.

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:04.100
The film weaves the series' different stories
together into its spooky tale of a group of

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:07.660
teenagers who discover the lost writings of
a very troubled little girl.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:12.020
Said writings have a way of leaping right
off the page...literally, as the book's spooky

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:14.220
contents start coming menacingly to life.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:18.500
Øvredal is no slouch when it comes to bringing
the scares, although, in keeping with its

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:21.900
target audience, the flick was found to be
more spooky than grisly.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.300
But most critics agreed that the director
had notched an admirable achievement.

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.560
Chicago Reader's Andrea Gronvall wrote:

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:32.090
"If there ever was a case of a film adaptation
improving on the original source material,

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:32.760
this is it."

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:37.840
In Ready or Not, a new bride is treated to
a murderous game of hide-and-seek at the estate

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:39.800
of her allegedly loving husband's family.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:44.360
The flick earned stellar reviews, most of
which praised its underlying themes of tension

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:48.900
between the rich and poor, as well as its
deft mix of comedy and jaw-dropping gore.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:50.740
Said Rolling Stone's Peter Travers:

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:54.951
"What a decadent blast to watch a comic takedown
of the rich done with the rude energy of a

00:03:54.951 --> 00:03:58.720
horror thriller and the courage of its own
manic anti-marriage convictions."

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.900
Lead actress Samara Weaving's performance
was also singled out for praise early and

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:07.140
often, but the definitive assessment of the
flick was handed down by Allen Adams of The

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:07.820
Maine Edge:

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:11.660
"In Ready or Not, you get a brutal and darkly
funny twist on horror movie tropes.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:15.120
You'll laugh, you'll cringe...and you'll probably
do both at the same time."

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:20.420
For six seasons, the British period drama
Downton Abbey kept audiences on both sides

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:21.730
of the pond enthralled.

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:26.070
The show ended in 2015, but the feature film
of the same name picked up right from where

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:26.660
it left off.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:31.380
Make no mistake: Downton Abbey is fan service
of the highest order, and while it might have

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:35.580
left the uninitiated puzzled, the flick was
a welcome gift for longtime viewers.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:37.600
As Matt Zoller-Seitz of RogerEbert.com wrote:

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:42.230
"The [three and a half] star rating at the
top of this review is not for people who don't

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:45.560
like Downton Abbey, have never seen it, or
grew tired of watching it.

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:50.000
The rating is for die-hards...the kinds of
viewers who will, I suspect, turn this movie

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:51.710
into an unexpected smash."

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:56.060
Lo and behold, he was right, illustrating
that Downton Abbey knew its audience better

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:58.060
than perhaps any film of 2019.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:04:59.160
"This is good.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.060
I thought something else was planned, but
it is excellent.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:05.440
So, a well done to old cuvay."

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:10.560
2019's most ambitious sci-fi drama saw an
astronaut journeying to the edge of the universe

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:11.820
to find his missing father.

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:16.320
If Ad Astra had fumbled its weighty premise
even the presence of Brad Pitt, one of Hollywood's

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.620
most bankable stars, might not have saved
it.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:23.520
Fortunately, co-writer and director James
Gray largely stuck the landing, and Pitt assisted

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.160
with a performance that ranks among the best
in his career.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.120
Ann Hornaday of The Washington Post praised
Pitt's performance while opining that the

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:33.090
visually impressive film's greatest strengths
lie in its themes.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:33.620
She wrote:

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:38.840
"It's a terrific ride, yes, but also a provocative
meditation on masculinity, the things we choose

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:43.710
to cherish or squander, and other eternal
verities of life that swirl, unresolved, while

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:46.020
our little blue marble continues to spin."

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:48.800
The great Leonard Maltin had even more praise,
writing:

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:52.310
"James Gray is one of our foremost, and most
original, filmmakers.

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:56.740
We already knew that Brad Pitt is an exceptional
leading man but this performance burnishes

00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:57.600
that reputation.

00:05:57.800 --> 00:05:59.960
The film is among this year's very best."

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:04.300
You probably knew right off the bat whether
Good Boys would be your cup of tea, or, more

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:05.900
accurately, your can of cheap beer.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:10.050
Still, for viewers who found the idea of a
trio of foul-mouthed tween boys stumbling

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:14.910
into a series of inappropriate situations
to be a formula for hilarity, the flick delivered,

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:15.400
big time.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:19.800
Good Boys' young leads were roundly praised
for their charming performances in a movie

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:24.490
that served up a surprising amount of warmth
alongside its crass comedic sensibility.

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:29.140
Chicago Sun-Times critic and former Roger
Ebert cohort Richard Roeper wrote:

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:34.410
"Even the most hardcore jokes have a good-natured
and even sweet larger context...For all its

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:37.040
wacky, gross-out, shock-ya humor, Good Boys
has a lot of heart."

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:38.140
"It's not a good burn!

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:40.820
His dad has two DUIs and has to take the train
to work.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:42.180
That's a good burn!"

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:46.520
It seems like only a matter of time before
the charismatic and hilarious Jillian Bell

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:47.880
achieves major stardom.

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:52.280
If the universe is a just place, Brittany
Runs a Marathon will be the final stop before

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:52.780
that destination.

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:56.900
The dramedy failed to set the box office on
fire, but it has all the makings of a future

00:06:56.900 --> 00:07:01.650
cult classic: it's a sometimes hysterical,
sometimes heartfelt examination of what it

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:03.560
takes to achieve true self-improvement.

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:07.900
It's also anchored by a lead performance which
suggests that this was the role Bell had been

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:08.820
waiting for all her life.

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:11.580
As Michael Phillips of the Chicago Tribune
put it:

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:13.730
"The movie succeeds because Bell succeeds.

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:18.090
It's gratifying to see a so-called character
actress with reliably deadly comic timing

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:21.380
loosen up, stretch out and learn what it means
to carry a movie."

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:25.920
The cast of The Farewell won't be terribly
well-known to American audiences, with one

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:31.050
glaring exception: Awkwafina, the rapper-turned-actress
who has had perhaps a hotter two-year run

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:32.240
than anyone in Hollywood.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:36.680
The dramedy deals with a large Chinese family
who throw together a wedding as an excuse

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:39.720
to get together when they discover that their
grandmother is dying.

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:44.480
Writer/director Lulu Wang puts her star through
her dramatic and comedic paces in service

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:48.650
of a film that deftly pulls off a trick which
is slowly starting to become more common in

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:53.260
Hollywood: telling a universally relatable
story from a specific cultural point of view.

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:58.580
The Farewell received near-universal praise,
with one common caveat: bring your tissues.

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:00.220
Total Film's Jamie Graham said:

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:04.760
"[It's] a film about family, with all of its
frets and foibles, fondness and fidelity.

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:08.860
It is heartfelt and beautifully observed,
so while everyone on screen is doing their

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:11.900
utmost to hold back tears, yours will come
unchecked."

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:16.320
Hustlers stars Jennifer Lopez as a veteran
stripper who trains her peers in the finer

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:19.919
points of her side job, drugging and ripping
off wealthy clients.

00:08:19.919 --> 00:08:24.320
Taking place against the backdrop of the 2008
financial collapse, the film was hailed for

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:28.050
its smart writing and directing, and the amazing
chemistry of its cast.

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:29.620
Inkoo Kang of Slate called Hustlers:

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:33.640
"An immediate entrant into the pantheon of
female friendship movies [and] a pretty much

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:34.500
perfect film."

00:08:34.649 --> 00:08:39.399
Many critics noted that those expecting a
pandering, empty exercise would be disappointed.

00:08:39.399 --> 00:08:41.279
As FilmBunker's Ciaran Kerr wrote:

00:08:41.279 --> 00:08:45.320
"What could have been a film weighed down
by its own agenda presented its main characters

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:50.240
as living, breathing, complex individuals,
shying away from the empty 'girl power' rhetoric

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:53.320
that a lot of Hollywood features try to feed
the masses as feminism.

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:56.180
Furthermore, it was entertaining and a pleasure
to watch."

00:08:56.760 --> 00:09:00.700
The Lighthouse is a black-and-white period
piece in which two lighthouse operators slowly

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:03.480
lose their minds while stationed on an isolated
island.

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:07.540
The film ended up being a lot more entertaining
than that sounds, thanks in large part to

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:11.380
the committed performances of its leads, Robert
Pattinson and Willem Dafoe.

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:15.780
Adam Graham of Detroit News drew an interesting
comparison in his assessment of the film.

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:16.280
He said:

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:21.200
"The Lighthouse is a starkly gorgeous black-and-white
head trip that is like staring into a blinding

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:25.220
light: you're hypnotized, you can't look away
and your eyes won't be the same after."

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:28.420
"Let Neptune strike ye dead, Winslow!

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:32.740
HAAAAARK!'

00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:36.880
Much had been written about The Irishman well
before its release, because the Netflix film

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:38.759
carries a pedigree unlike any other.

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:43.440
It's a late-career work from Martin Scorsese,
perhaps the greatest living film director,

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:46.740
which stars his longtime accomplices Robert
De Niro and Joe Pesci.

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:50.660
It's also got Al Pacino, with whom Scorsese
had never worked previously.

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:55.519
Finally, the film is based on the biography
of union leader Frank Sheeran, who is purported

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:58.320
to have had mob ties, and may have even killed
Jimmy Hoffa.

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:02.620
Critics welcomed it as an absolute tour de
force, one of Scorsese's best efforts in a

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:04.540
filmography full of masterpieces.

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:06.200
Rolling Stone's Peter Travers wrote:

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:10.760
"With The Irishman, America's greatest living
director creates his late-career masterpiece,

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:15.280
a deeply felt addition that vibrantly sums
up every landmark in his crime-cinema arsenal."

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:21.420
Veteran director James Mangold gave us a superhero
movie unlike any other with 2017's Logan,

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:26.340
and for his next trick, he decided to smoothly
shift gears into a period piece drama about

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:28.100
racecars and the men who drive them.

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:32.860
With Ford v Ferrari, Mangold had the added
bonus of working with Matt Damon and Christian

00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:37.269
Bale, two acting masters at imbuing their
characters with layers of emotional complexity.

00:10:37.269 --> 00:10:41.559
The resulting flick is a rousing historical
crowd-pleaser, with critics mostly agreeing

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:44.600
that the movie is every bit the well-oiled
machine that one would expect.

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:46.800
San Diego Reader's Matthew Lickona wrote:

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:50.860
"Mangold indulges himself with this tale of
American manhood, but he knows he can afford

00:10:50.860 --> 00:10:51.360
it.

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:54.280
He's got Bourne and Batman as brilliant, brawling
best buds."

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:58.259
More than a few critics opined that the film
is a touch too long, but that the amazing

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:01.920
chemistry between Damon and Bale carries it
through its more draggy stretches.

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:06.400
Its overall reception was best summarized
by Quad-City Times' Linda Cook who succinctly

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:06.900
wrote:

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:09.200
"Ford v Ferrari is a great ride."

00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:14.059
Tom Hanks appearing as Fred Rogers is about
the most spot-on piece of casting anyone could

00:11:14.059 --> 00:11:18.290
have ever hoped for, and the biopic A Beautiful
Day in the Neighborhood makes good on that

00:11:18.290 --> 00:11:18.780
potential.

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:22.340
At a time when we could all use a bit more
of the values that Rogers espoused, the movie

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:26.600
isn't content just to illustrate the late
children's show host's dedication to said

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:27.120
values.

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:30.560
Instead, it shows how those values inspired
everyone around him.

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:33.120
As RogerEbert.com's Monica Castillo, wrote:

00:11:33.129 --> 00:11:37.189
"[The movie] isn't so much a biopic as it
is a way to look at the way Mr. Rogers affected

00:11:37.189 --> 00:11:39.509
generations of children, young and grown.

00:11:39.509 --> 00:11:44.800
If his character seems too simple, it's likely
because that's how many people saw him, uncomplicated

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:49.120
and confined to his on-screen persona...That's
the lasting power of Mr. Rogers."

00:11:49.580 --> 00:11:54.660
Writer/director Rian Johnson wowed fans with
Knives Out, his inspired tribute to Agatha

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:56.180
Christie-style whodunits.

00:11:56.180 --> 00:12:00.639
With an all-star cast including Chris Evans,
Daniel Craig, Jamie Lee Curtis, and Michael

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:04.880
Shannon, the flick won over critics with its
crackerjack writing and playfully devious

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:05.380
vibe.

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:07.880
Eugene Weekly's Molly Templeton said of the
film:

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:12.720
"Johnson's vision translates a love for classic
murder mysteries into a kaleidoscopic ensemble

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:17.699
piece that twists and spins, using the structure
and tropes of mystery, and comedy, to expose

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:21.580
the endless ways humans justify being absolutely
rotten to each other."

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:26.660
Real-life whistleblowers have long provided
fodder for excellent cinematic dramatizations,

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:28.540
from All the President's Men to Michael Clayton.

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:32.800
The Mark Ruffalo vehicle Dark Waters falls
squarely into that tradition, telling the

00:12:32.809 --> 00:12:37.350
story of Robert Biliott, an attorney whose
20-year long pursuit of chemical corporation

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:40.060
DuPont upended his life in ways few could
imagine.

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:44.640
Directed by Oscar nominee Todd Haynes, the
film received critical kudos for spinning

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:48.600
an entertaining yarn from what could have
been the ingredients for a plodding procedural.

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:52.700
There was also plenty of praise for Ruffalo's
performance, which conclusively proves that

00:12:52.709 --> 00:12:54.389
the actor is more than just a Hulk.

00:12:54.389 --> 00:12:56.480
Richard Roeper of the Chicago Sun-Times wrote:

00:12:56.660 --> 00:13:00.420
"Ruffalo is a master at playing a certain
type of earnest character who often wears

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:04.410
a quizzical expression, not because he's slow
on the uptake, but because he's the smartest

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:07.980
person in the room and he has questions no
one else has even thought to ask."

00:13:07.980 --> 00:13:12.019
The film's true power was laid out nicely
by Dominic Griffin of Spectrum Culture:

00:13:12.019 --> 00:13:16.459
"Dark Waters is special because it's not about
dramatically building to an inevitable victory,

00:13:16.459 --> 00:13:19.980
but about the importance of fighting every
day anyway, knowing such a conclusion may

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:20.760
never come."

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:25.279
With any literary work that has been adapted
as many times as Louisa May Alcott's Little

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:28.580
Women, it's always worth asking if one more
is really necessary.

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:33.679
As illustrated by the 2019 adaptation, the
answer is yes, especially when an adaptation

00:13:33.679 --> 00:13:38.769
is written and directed by Greta Gerwig, the
filmmaker behind the Oscar-nominated 2017

00:13:38.769 --> 00:13:39.560
film Lady Bird.

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:44.119
Working with an all-star cast including the
likes of Saorise Ronan, Emma Watson, Florence

00:13:44.119 --> 00:13:48.999
Pugh, Laura Dern, and Meryl Streep, Gerwig
delivered a timeless, open-hearted adaptation

00:13:48.999 --> 00:13:52.529
that delighted long-time fans of the book
and new audiences alike.

00:13:52.529 --> 00:13:56.529
Lisa Johnson Mandell of At Home in Hollywood
singled out how the latest telling differs

00:13:56.529 --> 00:13:57.779
from all the rest, writing:

00:13:57.779 --> 00:14:01.629
"Little Women should be promoted with director
Greta Gerwig's name in the title.

00:14:01.629 --> 00:14:06.739
Her colorful, warm and creative touch is what
sets it apart from so many versions of the

00:14:06.739 --> 00:14:10.480
story, and so many other gritty, violent and
tiresome films of the day.

00:14:10.480 --> 00:14:11.750
It's simply a joy to watch."

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:15.340
The joy was definitely contagious; according
to Cinemayward's Joel Mayward:

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:19.799
"It's a warm hug of a film...Gerwig clearly
loves every one of these characters, and such

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:23.360
love flows from the screen to the audience
with generosity and grace."

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:28.980
That brothers Josh and Benny Safdie turned
in one of 2019's best isn't terribly surprising;

00:14:28.989 --> 00:14:33.559
their previous feature was the underrated
Good Time, a harrowing thriller which conclusively

00:14:33.559 --> 00:14:36.660
proved that star Robert Pattinson is the real
deal as an actor.

00:14:36.660 --> 00:14:40.839
The surprise in Uncut Gems is Adam Sandler,
who stars as the gambling-addicted jewelry

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:42.220
store owner Howard Ratner.

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:46.640
Critics were simply mesmerized by his performance,
which has been greeted by nearly universal

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:47.220
acclaim.

00:14:47.420 --> 00:14:49.420
The Sun-Times' Richard Roeper called the flick:

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:53.029
"Uncut Gems [is] one of the most authentic
deep-dives into the world of the gambling

00:14:53.029 --> 00:14:54.460
addict ever put on film.

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:59.720
Adam Sandler's performance as a New York jeweler
with a variety of voracious appetites...ranks

00:14:59.720 --> 00:15:03.500
right up there the work of Steve McQueen in
The Cincinnati Kid, Edward Norton and Matt

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:08.170
Damon in Rounders, George Segal and Elliott
Gould in California Split, and James Caan

00:15:08.170 --> 00:15:08.900
in The Gambler."

00:15:09.170 --> 00:15:13.880
Virtually all reviewers were stunned by Sandler's
work, and the supporting cast, which included

00:15:13.889 --> 00:15:18.899
the likes of Lakeith Stanfield, Kevin Garnett,
and Idina Menzel, were no slouches, either.

00:15:18.899 --> 00:15:21.779
Rob Dean of Daily Grindhouse arguably summed
it up best:

00:15:21.779 --> 00:15:26.360
"Uncut Gems is the best panic attack you'll
have all year...a brilliant character study,

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:30.560
an engaging story of various lowlifes, and
one of the best films of 2019."

00:15:31.040 --> 00:15:31.960
"This is me.

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:34.200
This is how I win."

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:38.600
When Joker was announced, it was easy to feel
like the flick was either going to be a disaster

00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:39.840
or a masterpiece.

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:43.800
Once the trailers were released, it quickly
became clear that the flick would be his homage

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:49.160
to gritty '70s character studies in general,
and to the works of Martin Scorsese in particular.

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:52.180
Director Todd Phillips' ace in the hole, though,
was his star.

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:57.260
Joaquin Phoenix and Phillips diligently crafted
an intense portrait of a very disturbed man,

00:15:57.260 --> 00:16:00.360
one which challenged the notion of what a
"comic book movie" could be.

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:05.420
Joker not only became the first R-rated film
to gross over a billion dollars worldwide,

00:16:05.420 --> 00:16:10.200
but it received 11 Academy Award nominations
- more than any other 2019 movie.

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:15.300
It may have divided critics, but not so much
audiences, who by and large saw the film for

00:16:15.310 --> 00:16:20.041
what it was: a masterclass in film studies,
with near-flawless acting, direction, and

00:16:20.041 --> 00:16:25.060
editing, and the greatest pure character study
that's been put to film in decades.

